FTL Travel

Overview: There are three forms of FTL travel in 2319, two of those are well developed and are in common use, the other is a brand new form of travel with only two working prototypes. While all three forms of FTL travel use quantum mechanics in combination with a Einstein-Rosen bridge in some manner, the three different technologies use these same basic principles to do wildly different ways. The largest factor dealing with the three technologies is the amount of energy needed to use them, and the size or complexity of the systems needed to implement them.

Stutterwarp: Stutterwarp was first developed in the mid 2050's and was the first form of FTL that humanity found a way to take from theory to practical. The Stutterwarp drive allows a ship to 'jump' a short distance by a combination of quantum tunneling and a very short Einstein-Rosen bridge, and it does this thousands of times per second. Depending on the complexity of a Stutterwarp drive in could make one jump in as little as every few nanoseconds. While the Stutterwarp drives of the 21st century were large, bulky, power hogs modern Stutterwarp drives can actually be made small enough to fit into drones and one man starships. The key limitation to Stutterwarps isn't power, its speed. No matter how much power is given into a Stutterwarp drive, the distance between jumps is relatively fixed, which means the only way to go faster is to jump more frequently, and in 2319 the very best speed humanity can get (with the most powerful computers) is around 10x the speed of light, with common Stutterwarp drives working in the 2x to 4x speed of light range. In the late 21st century and early 22nd century the Stutterwarp was an amazing piece of technology that allowed humanity to develop bases, industry, and research outposts throughout the Sol systems and even spread out to a very few close stars like those in the Alpha Centauri system, but if humanity was to ever become a truly interstellar species they would need more.

Jumpdrive: In 2125 the very first Jump-Drive, more commonly known as the 'Jumpdrive', was developed and in the following nearly two centuries has seen humanity spread out over a vast number of worlds, dozens of lightyears from humanities birthplace. While early jumpships, and commonly used jumpships in modern times, can only move up to around 8 lightyears in a single 'jump', the technology has developed to the point that some ships can 'jump' as far as 24 lightyears. The jumpdrive requires very extreme amounts of power to form a link between two points in space using quantum entanglement and form a very short lived Einstein-Rosen bridge through which the jumpship is basically push-pulled through forcefully. It is not an elegant form of FTL, but it is effective. The limiting factor to this technology is the power constraints. The amount of power required for a ship to brute force move itself in this manner is far more than even the most advanced fusion generators can produce, more than a dozen of the most advanced fusion generators can produces. So in order to produce the kind of power a ship needs to use its jumpdrive it must deploy a massive solar collector relatively close to a star and feed that power directly into the capacitor like jump coils for up to around a week, and at the end of that time release all the energy at once, like a controlled fusion bomb, within the jumpdrive. Another limited factor to this technology is the requirement that a jumpdrive cannot move more than its own mass AND the field it generates has a very limited range. This has led to jumpships being shaped like long tubes, within which the jumpdrive is housed, with large stutterwarp equipped 'dropships' attached to the exterior. Because it takes around a week to recharge the jumpship relatively near a star, and with a giant kilometers wide solar collector, the jumpships effectively become mobile platforms for the dropships, which will do their business within a star system over the week it takes to recharge and then rejoin the jumpship for the next jump.
 With humanity spreading further and further out from earth in sometimes uncontrolled ways, it can now take months for a ship to travel from the Sol system, home of the Earth, out to the distant fringes of human expansion using jumpships. While scientists are working for ways to implement faster and more versatile FTL technologies, so far the only way they have found to alleviate the growing travel times is the newest form of FTL travel.

Jumpgate: The very first practically working, large scale prototype, jumpgates were built around both Sol and Alpha Centauri in 2297. Over the next nearly two decades a project was developed to spread this new form of technology to key points within the human sphere of influence to create regional hubs linked directly to Sol, potentially cutting travel times throughout it from months down to weeks. A 'Jump-Gate', more commonly referred to only as a jumpgate is a massive piece of technology that includes a 1km diameter ring shaped primary structure referred to as the gate, and an even larger 100km diameter solar collector to power the gate. While a jumpdrive uses quantum entanglement and a Einstein-Rosen bridge to brute force move through space, the jumpgate uses two gates to do the same in a more controlled and refined manner, which allows an actual short-term wormhole to be formed between the two points through which any ship can pass through virtually instantaneously. At present this technology has a maximum range of around 120 lightyears, but the scientists involved with its development are quite certain that the only limitation to distance is power. In 2319 the very first missions in a century long over-arcing mission to seed human space with these jumpgates began, and within a century there should be hundreds of these gates built throughout the human controlled space.

Notes: There is one form of technology being developed that could almost make stutterwarp, jumpdrives, and jumpgates obsolete if anyone were to make it work on a practical scale. One such attempt to do so has been named the 'Ava-Drive' by its lead developer, but the power requirements mostly make this research a pipe dream until scientists can produce a power source such as functional anti-matter generators or vacuum-state energy, a functional 'warp drive' is just a pipe-dream.


